[A4 THE COBOUNDARY GATE — D64's C7, BOTH ROUTES, at five port conventions.  The question D64 left: can ANY substrate carry a transition class that is NOT a coboundary?]
  THE PORT CONVENTIONS FOR 3+-REGISTER EVENTS, DEFINED AND PRINTED (pin A4).
  An arbitration's registers are its PROPOSERS plus the newly minted version,
  and its role structure gives several natural orders.  All five are run:
    REG       — d64's canonical: the layer's own tuple order (proposers sorted,
                then the version).  Deliveries: (sender, receiver).
    REGA      — every register sorted by name (d64's alternative port order).
    ARBLOSE   — LOSERS first, then WINNERS, then the version: the conflict's
                own asymmetry used as the port order, which is what D64's
                residue 1 said an arbitration has and a delivery does not.
    ARBVFIRST — the VERSION first, then the proposers sorted: the minted wire
                read as port 0, a referee-style alternative that shifts every
                port index.
    COV       — the register-free surrogate (covering relation, cover-index
                ports), the only instrument also defined on sprinklings.
  TWO NATURAL CONVENTIONS COINCIDE WITH REG ON THIS FAMILY and are not run
  separately: initiator-first and winner-first, because the schedule always
  makes the initiator the sorted-first proposer.  That is a fact about the
  blueprint, printed here rather than hidden in a choice.

